.TH FCHOWNAT 2 "February 2026" "Substrate" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
fchownat, lchownat \- change ownership of file (relative to directory fd)
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library (libc, \-lc)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.B #include <fcntl.h>
.PP
.B #include <sys/types.h>
.PP
.B #include <sys/stat.h>
.PP
.BI "int fchownat(int dirfd, const char *pathname, uid_t uid, gid_t gid, int flag);"
.PP
.BI "int lchownat(int dirfd, const char *pathname, uid_t uid, gid_t gid, int flag);"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fchownat()
changes the ownership of the file referred to by a directory file descriptor
.IR dirfd ,
with the path specified as
.IR pathname .
If
.I pathname
is relative, it is interpreted relative to the directory referred to by
.IR dirfd .
If
.I pathname
is absolute,
.I dirfd
is ignored unless
.B AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
is set in
.IR flag .
The
.I uid
and
.I gid
arguments specify the new owner and group.
Passing \-1 leaves the respective ID unchanged.
.PP
.B lchownat()
is similar to
.B fchownat()
but always operates on the symbolic link itself, never on its target.
.PP
The
.I flag
argument is a bit mask formed by ORing the following constants:
.TP
.B AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
If
.I pathname
is a symbolic link, the operation applies to the link itself, not its target.
This is equivalent to
.BR lchown (2)
semantics for the final component.
.TP
.B AT_REMOVEDIR
When
.I pathname
names a directory and the operation is
.BR lchownat ,
allows deletion of the directory.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, zero is returned. On error, \-1 is returned.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EACCES
Search permission is denied for a component of the path prefix.
.TP
.B EBADF
.I dirfd
is not a valid file descriptor.
.TP
.B ELOOP
A loop was detected in symbolic links.
.TP
.B EINVAL
One of
.I uid
or
.I gid
was negative.
.TP
.B ENAMEXLERR
.I pathname
is too long.
.TP
.B ENOENT
The file does not exist, or a component of the path does not exist.
.TP
.B ENOTDIR
A component of the path prefix is not a directory.
.TP
.B EPERM
The effective user ID is not root, and the caller does not own the file.
.SH EXAMPLES
Change ownership of
.I file
in the directory referred to by
.I fd
to user 1000:
.IP
.BI "fchownat(fd, \"file\", 1000, \-1, 0)"
.PP
Change ownership of a symbolic link itself:
.IP
.BI "fchownat(fd, \"link\", uid, gid, AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW)"
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR intro (2),
.BR chown (1),
.BR lchown (2),
.BR fchown (2)
